  "slug": "feedback-network-4d18",
  "summary": "High-dimensional omics and clinical datasets are driving benchmarking and new selection methods for interpretable biomedical ML.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is selecting or constructing informative features from high-dimensional biomedical data before or alongside model training. EECFS applies ensemble causal feature selection with faster spouse discovery to molecular data. A semantics-based approach uses Gene Ontology structure to choose predictive gene subsets for clinical ML, benchmarked on 16 medical datasets. A benchmark of model-free sure independence screening methods on type 1 diabetes omics finds BcorSIS most effective. The GWAS preprint takes a related route, encoding high-order epistatic decision paths from CART as dummy variables to improve interaction detection. What is new is the incorporation of structured relations, namely causal dependencies, ontology semantics and tree-derived interaction paths, directly into feature construction and selection. megaMine sits adjacent to this thread, applying transparent rule-based mining to gene-cancer-drug evidence from PubMed, PubTator and Europe PMC.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-08-14"
}
